        <h2 id="vietnam-bangladesh">Vietnam &amp; Bangladesh: Volume, Cost &amp; Compliance Considerations</h2>

        <p>Vietnam and Bangladesh carry the bulk fleece programs for most Western brands, and the logic is simple. They deliver consistent quality at scale for less money than China</p>

        <p>Vietnam has moved well past basic cut-and-sew. Factories there handle bonded fleece, technical finishes, and multi-panel construction, with delivery dates you can plan a retail calendar around. Bangladesh remains the cost leader on high-volume basics: plain pullovers, standard 300 GSM fleece, simple left-chest embroidery, typically at 2,000 pieces or more per color</p>

        <p>Compliance usually decides the matter. European and North American retailers increasingly require social-compliance audits, and in some chemical-sensitive categories, environmental certification as well. Confirm which audit frameworks a factory actually holds, with report dates, before you commit tooling or deposits. Audit status drives both eligibility and price. Our overview of <a href="/our-services/">apparel compliance and certifications</a> walks through what each framework verifies in practice</p>

        <p>This region fits when your order is large, your design is stable, unit cost drives the decision, and you can live with longer production plus 25–35 days on the water. It does not fit when you need 200 pieces across three colorways next month. The MOQ math never works</p>

        <h2 id="portugal-turkey">Portugal, Turkey &amp; Near-Shore Options: Speed, Quality &amp; Smaller Runs</h2>

        <p>Near-shoring has quietly become the default for European brands that would rather pay more per piece than wait eight weeks for a container</p>

        <p>Portugal sits at the high end. Factories there specialize in heavyweight fleece at 400 GSM and above, garment-dyed and overdyed finishes, and the kind of seam and rib detailing that justifies a €150 retail price. Runs of 150–500 pieces per colorway are normal, and in-line QC is strong. Prices sit at the top of this list, and so does the construction ceiling</p>

        <p>Turkey occupies the middle ground. It combines competitive pricing with Turkish-spun cotton fleece from local mills, 7–14 day sampling, and 25–45 day production. For a brand running monthly drops into the EU, that cycle makes real replenishment possible instead of theoretical</p>

        <p>Both options buy you the same things: shorter freight legs, factory visits you can do in a day, faster reorders, and a country-of-origin story that holds up at a higher price point. If you compete on fit and finish rather than price, near-shore production tends to pay for itself through fewer markdowns</p>

        <h2 id="choosing-a-location">How to Choose the Right Manufacturing Location for Your Hoodie Project</h2>

        <p>Work through five variables in order. The first two eliminate most of the map on their own</p>

        <p><strong>1. Order quantity per colorway.</strong> Under 300 pieces, your realistic choices are China, Portugal, Turkey, or domestic production. Above 1,000, Vietnam and Bangladesh come into play</p>

        <p><strong>2. Target retail price and margin.</strong> A $120 hoodie and a $35 hoodie cannot share a supply chain. High-end construction belongs in Portugal or Turkey. Value basics belong in Bangladesh or Vietnam</p>

        <p><strong>3. Decoration complexity.</strong> All-over print, puff, chenille, or four embroidery placements narrow the field quickly. China leads; Turkey and Portugal can execute it at a higher price</p>

        <p><strong>4. Compliance requirements.</strong> If your retailer demands a specific audit, verify factory status before you order samples, not after you have approved a fit</p>

        <p><strong>5. Lead time tolerance.</strong> Budget 10–20 days for custom dyeing, 25–35 days for ocean freight, and a buffer for port congestion. With a fixed launch date, work backward from the floor set and let the calendar choose the country</p>

        <p>Use this matrix as a first filter:</p>

        <div class="ar-table-wrap"><table class="ar-table"><thead><tr><th>Your Priority</th><th>Recommended Location</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Small batch + complex decoration</td><td>China</td></tr><tr><td>Lowest unit cost at volume</td><td>Bangladesh</td></tr><tr><td>Balance of cost and complexity</td><td>Vietnam</td></tr><tr><td>Fast EU replenishment</td><td>Turkey</td></tr><tr><td>High-end quality, small runs</td><td>Portugal</td></tr><tr><td>"Made in USA" positioning</td><td>United States</td></tr></tbody></table></div>

        <p>Before you contact factories, build a proper tech pack and cost sheet. Our <a href="/how-to-find-a-hoodie-manufacturer/">guide to finding a hoodie manufacturer</a> covers what to prepare so the quotes you receive are actually comparable</p>

        <p>One more warning for this stage: a quote that looks 20% cheaper frequently loses that gap once you add freight, duty, sample fees, and a 3% defect allowance. Compare landed cost per sellable unit or do not compare at all</p>

        <h2 id="common-mistakes">Common Mistakes When Sourcing Hoodies Overseas</h2>

        <p>Most sourcing failures trace back to five avoidable errors</p>

        <ol class="ar-list"><li><strong>Chasing the lowest unit price.</strong> The cheapest quote usually carries a higher MOQ, thinner QC, or a longer ship date. Price without context tells you nothing</li><li><strong>Leaving MOQ to the end.</strong> Finding out about a 2,000-piece minimum after designing a five-color drop costs weeks of design time</li><li><strong>Skipping the pre-production sample.</strong> Lab dips and fit samples surface problems while they still cost $200 to fix instead of $20,000</li><li><strong>Taking certifications on trust.</strong> Claims cost nothing. Ask for the audit report, check the issue date, check the scope</li><li><strong>Underestimating logistics and duty.</strong> FOB is a negotiating number. Landed cost is what hits your P&amp;L</li></ol>

        <p>A structured supplier evaluation catches all five before money moves, which is precisely what experienced sourcing support is for</p>

